For our KYC platform, I would organize this as a **document and evidence catalog covering identity, legal existence, ownership, authority, business activity, financial resources, tax status, and ongoing due diligence**.

There is no universal document checklist. Requirements depend on jurisdiction, customer type, product, delivery channel, ownership structure, and risk. Some information can be verified through authoritative data or digital identity services without requesting a document from the customer. The catalog below is a comprehensive universe of potential evidence—not a requirement to collect everything. [FATF Recommendations](https://www.fatf-gafi.org/en/publications/Fatfrecommendations/Fatf-recommendations.html), [HKMA account-opening guidance](https://www.hkma.gov.hk/eng/smart-consumers/account-opening/information-required)

I use “others” here to include periodic reviews, changes in circumstances, EDD, transaction investigations, and account closure.

**1. Individual identity and personal particulars**

These apply to individual customers and, where relevant, business owners, controllers, directors, trustees, representatives, and authorized signatories.

| Document family | Examples | Typical purpose |
|---|---|---|
| Passport | Ordinary passport; official or diplomatic passport where accepted | Identity, nationality, date of birth |
| National identity document | National ID card; government electronic ID credential | Identity and government identifier |
| Driving licence | Government-issued photocard driving licence | Identity; sometimes address |
| Residence and immigration evidence | Residence permit; permanent-resident card; visa; electronic immigration-status confirmation | Residency or immigration status where relevant |
| Alternative travel documents | Refugee travel document; stateless-person travel document; government-issued travel certificate | Identity for applicants without conventional passports |
| Civil registration documents | Birth certificate; citizenship certificate; naturalization certificate; family register | Supporting identity, nationality, parentage |
| Name-change documents | Marriage certificate; divorce decree; deed poll; court order; official name-change certificate | Reconcile current and previous names |
| Other accepted government identification | Military ID; voter ID; other locally recognized credentials | Alternative or supplementary identification |
| Tax identifier evidence | Tax registration card; tax authority letter; government identifier confirmation | Verify tax or other identifying number |
| Signature evidence | Specimen signature card; signed application; accepted electronic-signature record | Establish signing specimen and attribution |

**Acceptance is document-, channel-, and jurisdiction-specific.** A birth certificate or tax card, for example, may support identification without being sufficient on its own. [FFIEC identification guidance](https://bsaaml.ffiec.gov/manual/AssessingComplianceWithBSARegulatoryRequirements/01)

**2. Individual address and residence evidence**

| Document family | Examples |
|---|---|
| Utility statements | Electricity, gas, water, fixed-line telephone, or other accepted utility bill |
| Financial statements | Bank, building society, credit union, credit card, or mortgage statement |
| Government correspondence | Tax assessment; municipal tax bill; benefits letter; official residence certificate |
| Housing evidence | Tenancy agreement; lease; property title; landlord confirmation where accepted |
| Employment or education evidence | Employer accommodation letter; university accommodation or enrollment letter |
| Supported accommodation evidence | Care-home letter; shelter confirmation; recognized support-organization letter |
| Household relationship evidence | Host declaration plus host’s address evidence and relationship confirmation, where accepted |
| Address-bearing identity document | Accepted national ID, residence document, or driving licence |

Do not hard-code a universal “three-month validity” rule. Freshness, acceptable issuers, and whether one document can establish both identity and address vary by institution and policy. [HSBC identification examples](https://www.hsbc.co.uk/help/banking-made-easy/help-us-identify-you/), [TSB identification examples](https://www.tsb.co.uk/current-accounts/faqs/identity.html)

**3. Individual occupation, income, and economic profile**

These may support understanding the relationship, EDD, or product-specific assessments.

| Circumstance | Potential documents |
|---|---|
| Employed | Employment contract; employer letter; payslips; salary certificate; annual earnings statement |
| Self-employed or freelance | Business registration; professional licence; service contracts; invoices; tax returns; business accounts |
| Business owner | Company financial statements; ownership evidence; dividend vouchers; distribution statements |
| Retired | Pension award letter; pension statements; retirement-benefit statement |
| Student | Enrollment letter; scholarship or grant award; sponsor declaration |
| Financially supported | Sponsor or family-support declaration; relationship evidence; supporting transfer records |
| Receiving benefits | Government benefit award or payment statement |
| Receiving rental income | Property ownership evidence; tenancy agreements; rental statements |
| Investor | Brokerage, custody, investment portfolio, or fund statements |

Income evidence collected for lending affordability should have its own purpose classification, even when the same document also supports KYC.

**4. Individual source of funds and source of wealth**

These should be separate evidence requirements:

- **Source of funds:** how the money used for a particular transaction or relationship was obtained.
- **Source of wealth:** how the person accumulated their overall wealth.

A statement showing money arriving from another account establishes a transfer path; it may not establish the underlying economic source. [AUSTRAC source-of-funds guidance](https://www.austrac.gov.au/industry-and-business/obligations-and-guidance/your-amlctf-program/customer-due-diligence/source-funds-and-source-wealth)

| Claimed source | Potential supporting documents |
|---|---|
| Employment savings | Historical payslips; earnings statements; tax returns; savings-account history |
| Business profits | Audited accounts; tax returns; ownership records; dividend or distribution records |
| Sale of a business | Share or asset purchase agreement; completion statement; payment evidence |
| Property sale | Sale contract; conveyancer’s completion statement; ownership record; receipt of proceeds |
| Securities or investment sale | Broker contract notes; redemption confirmations; portfolio and settlement statements |
| Inheritance | Grant of probate; letters of administration; relevant will provisions; estate distribution statement |
| Gift | Gift deed or declaration; donor identity and relationship evidence; donor source-of-funds evidence where needed |
| Loan | Executed loan agreement; lender details; disbursement confirmation; bank records |
| Divorce settlement | Court order; settlement agreement; payment evidence |
| Insurance payout | Policy details; claim settlement letter; payment confirmation |
| Compensation or legal settlement | Court judgment; settlement agreement; solicitor’s confirmation |
| Pension withdrawal | Pension-provider statement; withdrawal or lump-sum confirmation |
| Asset sale | Vehicle, artwork, jewellery, or other asset sale agreement; ownership and payment evidence |
| Trust distribution | Trustee resolution; distribution statement; relevant trust and funding evidence |
| Digital assets | Exchange statements; acquisition and trade history; wallet transaction records; evidence of original purchase funds |
| Other documented windfall | Official award or prize confirmation; payment evidence; explanation of circumstances |

The evidence package should reconcile **economic origin, amount, ownership, dates, and movement of funds**. [HMRC source-of-funds and wealth guidance](https://www.gov.uk/hmrc-internal-manuals/anti-money-laundering-guidance-for-supervised-businesses/amlg11630), [Kraken evidence examples](https://support.kraken.com/articles/source-of-wealth-or-source-of-funds-documents)

**5. Business legal existence and constitution**

| Document family | Examples |
|---|---|
| Formation | Certificate of incorporation; certificate of formation; registration certificate |
| Constitutional documents | Memorandum and articles of association; articles of organization; bylaws; charter; constitution |
| LLC governance | Operating agreement; member agreement |
| Partnership governance | Partnership deed; partnership agreement; limited-partnership or LLP agreement |
| Official registry evidence | Company registry extract; business register report; commercial register extract |
| Current legal status | Certificate of good standing; certificate of existence; status certificate |
| Current officeholders | Certificate of incumbency; register of directors; secretary’s certificate |
| Business registration | Business registration certificate; trade-name or assumed-name registration |
| Foreign establishment | Branch registration; foreign-company registration; representative-office approval |
| Legal changes | Change-of-name certificate; conversion, continuation, or re-domiciliation documents |
| Reorganization | Merger certificate; demerger documents; restructuring agreements |
| Distress or termination | Insolvency order; administrator or liquidator appointment; dissolution or restoration documents |

These establish different facts. A certificate of incorporation establishes formation; it does not necessarily establish current ownership, active trading, or present good standing. [HKMA corporate-document examples](https://www.hkma.gov.hk/media/eng/doc/key-functions/banking-stability/consumer-corner/english/1-Account_Opening_and_Maintenance-English.pdf)

**6. Business address, operating presence, and legitimacy**

| Document family | Examples |
|---|---|
| Registered office | Registry record; registered-office confirmation |
| Operating premises | Commercial lease; property title; premises utility bill |
| Occupancy arrangements | Serviced-office agreement; coworking agreement; registered-agent agreement |
| Operating licences | Municipal trade licence; establishment permit; sector-specific operating licence |
| Trading evidence | Customer contracts; supplier agreements; invoices; purchase orders; delivery records |
| Market presence | Product catalog; business website; brochures; marketplace or merchant profile |
| Operational capacity | Payroll summaries; organization chart; warehouse or facility records, where relevant |
| Independent verification | Site-visit report; premises photographs; independent business-verification report |
| New business | Business plan; financial projections; startup funding commitments; initial contracts |

A registered-agent address and an operating address should be represented separately.

**7. Ownership and beneficial ownership**

| Document family | Examples |
|---|---|
| Customer ownership declaration | Beneficial ownership certification; ownership questionnaire |
| Ownership chart | Signed or certified group chart showing each ownership layer |
| Shareholder records | Register of members; shareholder register; capitalization table |
| Ownership instruments | Share certificates; membership-interest records; partnership-interest schedules |
| Official ownership records | Beneficial ownership register extract; corporate filings; annual return |
| Ownership changes | Share transfer forms; subscription agreements; allotment records; acquisition agreements |
| Intermediate entities | Formation, registry, and ownership documents for entities in the chain |
| Nominee arrangements | Nominee declaration; declaration of trust; nominee agreement |
| Custodial holdings | Custodian statement; depositary or nominee holding confirmation |
| Listed-company ownership | Stock-exchange filings; annual report; substantial-shareholder disclosures |
| Bearer interests | Custody or immobilization confirmation; applicable supporting declarations |

The system must support both direct and indirect ownership evidence. A signed ownership chart is an assertion to corroborate, rather than automatic proof of every relationship.

**8. Control, management, and authority to act**

Ownership, control, and authority to operate an account are separate concepts.

| Purpose | Potential documents |
|---|---|
| Identify management | Director register; officer list; management organization chart |
| Establish control rights | Shareholder agreement; voting agreement; reserved-matters schedule; veto or appointment-right provisions |
| Authorize account opening | Board resolution; partner resolution; trustee resolution; governing-body minutes |
| Establish signing authority | Account mandate; authorized-signatory list; signing limits |
| Delegated authority | Power of attorney; agency agreement; delegated-authority letter |
| Verify appointment | Director, trustee, officer, or representative appointment documents |
| Verify representatives | Identity and relevant address evidence for authorized persons |
| Confirm continuing authority | Updated incumbency certificate; renewed mandate; confirmation of non-revocation |

Banks commonly request both evidence of the representative’s identity and evidence of their authority. [HKMA account-opening guidance](https://www.hkma.gov.hk/eng/smart-consumers/account-opening/information-required)

**9. Business financial position and funding**

| Document family | Examples |
|---|---|
| Annual financial reporting | Audited financial statements; unaudited accounts; annual report |
| Current financial reporting | Management accounts; interim statements; cash-flow statements |
| Banking history | Business bank statements; banking reference where appropriate |
| Tax evidence | Corporate tax returns; tax assessments; VAT/GST or sales-tax filings |
| Initial capital | Capital contribution records; subscription agreements; funding-account statements |
| Parent or shareholder funding | Intercompany loan; shareholder loan; parent funding commitment |
| External borrowing | Loan agreement; facility letter; lender confirmation |
| Equity investment | Investment agreement; fundraising documents; investor subscription records |
| Grants and subsidies | Grant award; subsidy approval; disbursement confirmation |
| Revenue support | Sales ledger; merchant settlement reports; major contracts and invoices |
| Liquidity or asset realization | Asset-sale agreement; investment redemption statement; settlement records |

These documents can support business understanding and funding provenance. Financial health assessment remains a separate use when performed for credit underwriting. [HSBC business funding examples](https://www.business.hsbc.com.hk/-/media/media/hong-kong/pdfs/form-download-centre/limited-company-and-general-partnership-established-overseas557-1-en.pdf)

