These are alternative structural packages, selected according to the legal form. [HSBC entity-specific onboarding examples](https://www.business.hsbc.com.my/solutions/account-opening)

**11. Trusts and similar legal arrangements**

Trusts need their own document model.

Potential documents include:

- Trust deed or other constituting instrument.
- Amendments, supplemental deeds, and restatements.
- Trust registration certificate or registry extract, where applicable.
- Trustee appointment, retirement, and replacement deeds.
- Settlor or grantor details and identity evidence.
- Trustee details and identity or entity documents.
- Protector, appointor, enforcer, or equivalent role documents.
- Beneficiary schedule or description of beneficiary classes.
- Documents establishing powers of appointment, revocation, distribution, or other control.
- Relevant letters of wishes, where necessary and appropriate.
- Trust asset and contribution schedules.
- Source-of-funds and wealth evidence for relevant contributors.
- Trust accounts, tax documents, and distribution records.
- Trustee resolution and account mandate.
- Underlying company ownership documents where the trust owns companies.

FATF’s trust framework distinguishes settlors, trustees, protectors, beneficiaries or beneficiary classes, and other persons exercising ultimate effective control. [FATF glossary](https://www.fatf-gafi.org/en/pages/fatf-glossary.html), [FATF legal-arrangements guidance](https://www.fatf-gafi.org/en/publications/Fatfrecommendations/Guidance-Beneficial-Ownership-Transparency-Legal-Arrangements.html)

**12. Tax residency and reporting status**

These are often collected alongside KYC but should be a distinct requirement domain.

| Document family | Examples |
|---|---|
| Individual tax residency | Tax-residency self-certification; tax residence certificate |
| Entity tax residency | Entity self-certification; tax residence certificate |
| Controlling persons | Controlling-person tax-residency self-certification |
| Tax identifiers | TIN evidence; business tax registration; explanation for missing TIN |
| Entity classification | Financial institution, active/passive entity, or other applicable classification declaration |
| US-person certification | Form W-9 where applicable |
| Foreign individual status | Form W-8BEN where applicable |
| Foreign entity status | Form W-8BEN-E where applicable |
| Intermediary status | Form W-8IMY and relevant supporting statements |
| Other withholding classifications | Forms W-8ECI or W-8EXP where applicable |
| Financial institution status | GIIN information and verification evidence where relevant |
| Changed circumstances | Updated certification; discrepancy explanation; supporting status documents |

CRS controlling-person status, tax-form beneficial ownership, and AML beneficial ownership should not be treated as interchangeable concepts. [OECD controlling-person self-certification](https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/topics/policy-issue-focus/aeoi/crs_controlling_persons_self-cert_form.pdf), [IRS withholding certificate forms](https://www.irs.gov/individuals/international-taxpayers/withholding-certificate-forms-under-irc-sections-1441-1464)

**13. Relationship purpose and expected activity**

Much of this begins as a form or structured declaration, with supporting documents requested when warranted.

| Information being established | Potential evidence |
|---|---|
| Purpose of relationship | Account application; purpose declaration; relationship questionnaire |
| Products and services | Product application; service agreement |
| Expected activity | Expected transaction volumes, values, currencies, and frequency declaration |
| Geographic exposure | Countries-of-operation declaration; major trading-market information |
| Counterparties | Major customer and supplier lists; contracts supporting material relationships |
| Cash activity | Cash-use explanation; cash-handling model; operating records |
| International payments | Import/export profile; trade contracts; payment-flow explanation |
| Third-party funds | Agency, client-money, escrow, collection, or payment-processing agreements |
| Group treasury | Group structure; treasury policy; cash-pooling and intercompany agreements |
| Initial deposit | Funding declaration and supporting source-of-funds package |

**14. Sector-specific and institutional due diligence**

These are conditional extensions, not baseline requests for every company.

| Sector/customer | Potential additional documents |
|---|---|
| Banks and correspondent institutions | Banking licence; regulatory status; ownership and management information; Wolfsberg CBDDQ/FCCQ; AML/sanctions control documentation; independent assurance summaries |
| Money services and remittance businesses | Registration or licence; agent network; payment corridors; AML program; monitoring and audit evidence |
| Payment institutions and processors | Regulatory authorization; payment-flow diagrams; merchant underwriting policies; settlement and safeguarding arrangements |
| Virtual asset service providers | Registration or licence; custody model; AML and travel-rule controls; wallet and asset provenance records |
| Investment funds | Offering memorandum; prospectus; constituting agreement; manager, administrator, and custodian appointments; investor/control information as applicable |
| Investment managers and brokers | Regulatory authorization; mandate; client-money arrangements; control documentation |
| Insurance businesses | Regulatory licence; ownership; relevant distribution and control arrangements |
| Professional firms | Practising certificates; professional registration; partnership documents; client-account mandate |
| Charities and NGOs | Donor and grant evidence; program descriptions; beneficiaries or delivery partners; geographic operations; disbursement arrangements |
| Gambling operators | Gambling licence; payment model; AML controls; relevant operating agreements |
| Precious metals or stones dealers | Dealer licence where applicable; supplier and provenance records; trading evidence |
| Import/export businesses | Trade licences; customs registrations; major contracts; shipping and transaction evidence |
| Regulated goods businesses | Sector permits; applicable import/export authorizations; end-use evidence |
| Real-estate businesses | Professional registration; project and property records; relevant agency agreements |
| Marketplaces and platforms | Seller onboarding model; underlying merchant profile; fund-flow and payout arrangements |

Correspondent banking has a dedicated due-diligence framework; a completed questionnaire is part of an assessment, not a complete substitute for one. [AUSTRAC correspondent-banking guidance](https://www.austrac.gov.au/due-diligence-correspondent-banking-relationships), [Wolfsberg CBDDQ guidance](https://dev.wolfsberg-group.org/resources/correspondent-banking/129)

**15. Enhanced due diligence, screening clarification, and investigations**

| Trigger | Potential additional documents |
|---|---|
| PEP relationship | Public-role declaration; official appointment evidence; wealth explanation; relevant asset declarations |
| Family or close-associate connection | Relationship declaration and corroborating evidence where necessary |
| Screening identity ambiguity | Additional identity document; date/place-of-birth evidence; aliases or name-history documents |
| Complex ownership | Additional registry filings; control agreements; nominee disclosures; legal explanation |
| Conflicting evidence | Updated authoritative records; issuer confirmation; certified explanation |
| Adverse media | Customer response; court judgment; case disposition; regulator notice; other reliable contextual evidence |
| Unusual transaction | Transaction explanation; contract; invoice; delivery evidence; underlying funding records |
| Third-party payment | Payer/payee relationship evidence; agency agreement; commercial rationale |
| Sanctions-related authorization | Relevant licence, exemption, or authorization and its conditions |
| Unexplained wealth | Expanded wealth chronology and corroborating financial or legal records |
| Suspected document fraud | Issuer verification; replacement original; independent authenticity assessment |

Requests during investigations need controlled wording and routing. Internal suspicion records and regulatory reporting material belong in restricted institutional records, not in the ordinary customer document checklist.

